> Climate impacts have been increasing rapidly across the globe, with consequences for lives and livelihoods. The occurrence of catastrophic floods, wildfires, and heat waves is rising, while sea level rise threatens coastal communities and species and habitats are in peril in every country.
> Economics has an important perspective to offer on these risks. Whether climate impacts affect natural resources, industries, or human wellbeing, economics offers a consistent scheme to estimate the value of these losses to society. By situating all climate impacts into a common welfare framework alongside the costs and benefits of adaptation, mitigation, and economic growth, an economic assessment can support decision-making. Two further elements distinguish this approach: an explicit accounting of uncertainty, and a framework that highlights how changes in policy result in changes in outcomes.
> A recent study, Drivers of the country-level social cost of carbon (Rising 2024), assesses the economic risks of climate change across the globe. To do so, the study expands a climate-economy model, PAGE (Policy Analysis of the Greenhouse Effect). This model includes a sophisticated model of the climate, the costs of greenhouse gas mitigation, warming effects on both market outcomes and non-market, sea-level rise and storm surge, the potential for adaptation across these, the risk of global economic catastrophe, and some climate tipping points. The study takes this model to the sub-national level, including considering the role of international trade. The results provide actionable insights on the scale of global and national risks and what drives them.
> Who is at risk
> Many of the world’s poorest countries face the greatest climate risks, considering their high vulnerability, reliance on outdoor labor and natural resources, and, in many cases, exposure to already high temperatures. Within both poor and rich countries, poorer populations are also more highly exposed and vulnerable to climate-driven events.
> Analyses that focus on monetary losses undervalue the risks faced by poor communities. While the monetary loss of a flooded vacation mini-mansion is greater than the loss for a working-class home, the long-term disruption to working class household is greater, and the need for proactive public action needs to be focused on these communities.
> For national-level decision-makers (Ministries of Finance, risk managers), we quantify the effective increase in national-level risk after accounting for disparities in vulnerability and income. The largest increases in risk are across Southern Africa and the Middle East, reaching up to 60% increases in risk, but these effects also matter for OECD countries which show 10 – 20% increases. Addressing the risks faced by vulnerable groups can have macroeconomic benefits.
