> While global temperatures rise gradually, the events that strike communities and undermine economies are extreme and unexpected. A central loss estimate of 10% of GDP per year (which matches the evidence for 3 °C)  could result from some years with no damages, combined with years of catastrophic failures. Pursuing current policies put us on track for 3.7 °C in 2100 (15% reduction in global welfare), but includes a 1-in-20 chance that temperatures in 2100 will be above 4.9 °C (resulting in 30% reduction in welfare). Across the uncertainty in central damages, any level of temperature could result in losses more than twice as high as the ones described above.
> The decision-making process for such “acute” events and poor outcomes differs from chronic trends. When extremes are shrouded in uncertainty, the worst-case outcomes are justified in becoming the central drivers of decisions.
> Globally, a year of emissions is expected to produce $13.9 trillion in damages (considering the discounted stream of impacts imposed in the coming centuries). However, due to uncertainty in the climate system, in economic growth, and in the damage models, there is a 1-in-4 chance that it results in $46 trillion, over 3 times greater. There is a 13% chance that damages from emissions next year equal the whole productivity of the economy.
> Local decisions
